Decode 10-bit AV1 to LSB-aligned P010

The H713's AV1 core decodes 10-bit AV1 bit-exactly, but writes it in P010's
layout with each sample in bits 9:0 of its 16-bit word -- it has no way to
MSB-align them. Local kernel patch 0150 names that: V4L2_PIX_FMT_P010_LSB
('PL10') on the capture queue, and DRM_FORMAT_P010 with the new
DRM_FORMAT_MOD_ALLWINNER_LSB10 modifier for scanout; the H713 video plane
takes it directly. So 10-bit surfaces are real 10-bit surfaces here, unlike
HEVC Main10 on cedrus, which is served 8-bit.

VA-API has one AV1 profile for both depths, so the config claims YUV420 and
YUV420_10, the pixel-format attribute offers NV12 and P010, and the depth is
decided when the surfaces are created: ffmpeg asks for YUV420_10 for a 10-bit
sequence. hantro then has to be told before the capture format is looked for,
because it lists only the formats of its current depth; and after any OUTPUT
S_FMT, which resets it to 8 bits. A sequence control carrying only the depth
does that, as the HEVC SPS does for cedrus (0006). The configured depth joins
the geometry in deciding when the queues must be renegotiated.

Zero-copy: the export descriptor says P010 + LSB10, truthfully at the DRM
level, and mpv's drm_prime path hands the modifier to
drmModeAddFB2WithModifiers unchanged.

CPU readback: vaDeriveImage here is a copy, not a mapping, so both it and
vaGetImage hand out true P010 -- the copy is shifted into place (<<6). P010
joins the image formats, and an 8-bit image of a 10-bit surface (or the
reverse) is refused rather than misread.

